-- title:
--   Shift class of a regluing depends only on the Čech class
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and a commutative Artinian local ring $B$ whose residue field $k = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,B$ is algebraically closed, together with a commutative $B$-algebra $B_1$.
--
--   *Small-extension data.* The structure map $B \to B_1$ is surjective (`hπ`), its kernel $I$ is a nilpotent ideal (`hker`), and $I \cdot \mathfrak m_B = 0$ (`hsmall`), so that $B \to B_1$ is a small extension; moreover $I \le \mathfrak m_B$ (`hI`). The image of $q$ in $B$ is nilpotent (`hq`).
--
--   *The object over $B_1$ and its formal law.* There are a scheme $A_1$ with a morphism $f_1 : A_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} B_1$, a relative group law $L_1$ on $f_1$ (functorial multiplication, unit and inverse on $B_1$-points, with associativity, unit and inverse laws and naturality in the test scheme), the hypothesis `hc₁` that $L_1$ is commutative, and the bundle `h₁` asserting that $f_1$ is smooth and proper, has connected fibres and admits some relative group law. Further, $F$ is a commutative two-dimensional formal group law over $B$, and $\theta_1$ is a system of formal coordinates for $f_1$ in dimension $2$, i.e. an assignment, to each $B_1$-algebra $B'$ and each pair $s \in (B')^2$, of a point of $f_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} B'$; the hypothesis `hθ₁` states that $\theta_1$ is a system of formal coordinates for $L_1$ with formal group $F \otimes_B B_1$, in the sense of `RelativeGroupLaw.IsFormalCoordinates`: compatibility with $B_1$-algebra maps applied to nilpotent parameter tuples, and, for every $B_1$-algebra $B'$ and every ideal $J$ with $J^{n+1} = 0$, the fact that $\theta_1$ restricts to a bijection between tuples with entries in $J$ and the points of $f_1$ which are infinitesimal along $J$ (i.e. become the unit after reduction modulo $J$), carrying the truncated formal addition $F$ to the multiplication of $L_1$. The hypothesis `hinf₁` requires that for every $B_1$-algebra $C$, every $m$ and every point $P$ of $f_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with $q^m P$ equal to the unit (iterated multiplication `nsmulPt`), there is a nilpotent ideal $J \subseteq C$ along which $P$ is infinitesimal. The hypothesis `hheight` requires that for every field $k'$ and every surjective ring homomorphism $\rho : B \to k'$ the quotient of $k'[[X_1,X_2]]$ by the ideal generated by the two components of the $q$-th iterate series $(F \otimes_\rho k')^{[q]}$ (`nthSeries` at $q$) has $k'$-dimension $q^4$.
--
--   *The module realising the kernel.* $V$ is a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space, also a $B$-module compatibly with the $k$-action and with commuting left and right $k$-actions, and $\iota : V \to B$ is an injective $B$-linear map whose image is exactly $I = \ker(B \to B_1)$, viewed as a $B$-submodule (`hι`, `hιI`).
--
--   *The base deformation and its charts.* $D_0$ is a bare deformation of $(f_1, L_1)$ to $B$: a scheme $D_0.A$ with a morphism $D_0.f$ to $\operatorname{Spec} B$, a commutative relative group law $D_0.L$ on it, the smoothness/properness/connected-fibres bundle, and a morphism $D_0.g : A_1 \to D_0.A$ making $A_1$ the pullback of $D_0.f$ along $\operatorname{Spec} B_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ and compatible with the two group laws; $D_0.f$ is assumed separated. Furthermore $\mathcal U$ is a finite ordered affine open cover of $D_0.A$, $i_0$ one of its indices, and $e_0 : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal U.U\,i_0$ a section whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of $D_0.L$ (`he₀`); likewise $e_1$ is a $k$-point of the chart indexed by $i_0$ in the cover $\mathcal U_k$ of the special fibre $A_k = D_0.A \times_{\operatorname{Spec} B} \operatorname{Spec} k$ obtained by pulling $\mathcal U$ back, whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of the base-changed group law $D_0.L \otimes k$ (`he₁`). For each $s \in \mathcal U.\mathrm{Idx}\,1$ — that is, each strictly increasing pair of indices, with $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$ the intersection of the two corresponding opens — $\sigma_s$ is a ring isomorphism $k \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s) \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } \Gamma(A_k, (\mathcal U_k).\mathrm{inter}\,s)$, normalised by `hσ₁` (on elements $1 \otimes x$ it is the pullback of $x$ along the projection $A_k \to D_0.A$, restricted to the smaller open) and `hσ₂` (on elements $a \otimes 1$ it is the structure map of $k$).
--
--   *Coordinates on $D_0$.* $G_0$ is a deformation of $F \otimes_B B_1$ to $B$, i.e. a two-dimensional formal group law $G_0.F$ over $B$ with $G_0.F \otimes_B B_1 = F \otimes_B B_1$, and $G_0.F$ is commutative; $\theta_0$ is a system of formal coordinates for $D_0.f$ with $D_0.L.\mathrm{IsFormalCoordinates}\ G_0.F\ \theta_0$ (`hθ₀`) which lifts $\theta_1$, meaning that for every $B$-algebra which is also an $S$-algebra compatibly and every tuple of nilpotent parameters, the corresponding point of $f_1$ followed by $D_0.g$ is the corresponding point of $D_0.f$ (`hl₀`).
--
--   *The two coordinate cocycles.* Write $\mathcal O_k$ for the $O$-module presheaf `OModulePresheaf.unit` of the structure map $A_k \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, whose value on an open $U$ is $\Gamma(A_k, U)$ with the evident restrictions, and $\check C^i(\mathcal U_k, \mathcal O_k)$ for its Čech $i$-cochains on $\mathcal U_k$, with differentials $d^i$. Then $c$ and $c'$ are elements of the $k$-submodule of point derivations $\mathrm{Algebra.PointDerivations}$ of $\Gamma(A_k, (\mathcal U_k).U\,i_0)$ at the evaluation homomorphism determined by the unit point $e_1$, with values in $\operatorname{Hom}_k(V^\vee, \check C^1(\mathcal U_k, \mathcal O_k))$; that is, $k$-linear maps $D$ with $D(ab) = \mathrm{ev}(a) D(b) + \mathrm{ev}(b) D(a)$. The hypotheses `hc` and `hc'` state that for every section $a$ over that chart and every $\xi \in V^\vee$ the $1$-cochains $c(a)(\xi)$ and $c'(a)(\xi)$ lie in the kernel of $d^1$, i.e. are Čech $1$-cocycles.
--
--   *Regluing data.* $\tau$ and $\tau'$ assign to each $s \in \mathcal U.\mathrm{Idx}\,1$ an automorphism of the scheme $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$, and $D, D'$ are bare deformations of $(f_1, L_1)$ to $B$ with $D_0.\mathrm{IsRegluingBy}\ \mathcal U\ \tau\ D$ and $D_0.\mathrm{IsRegluingBy}\ \mathcal U\ \tau'\ D'$ (`hD`, `hD'`): each $\tau_s$ is a morphism over $D_0.f$ and fixes the restriction of $D_0.g$ to $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$, and there is a family of open immersions $\mathcal U.U\,i \to D.A$ over $D_0.f$, jointly surjective on points, compatible with $D_0.g$, and such that on each overlap the inclusion into $\mathcal U.U(s_0)$ followed by its immersion agrees with $\tau_s$ followed by the inclusion into $\mathcal U.U(s_1)$ followed by its immersion. The hypotheses `hτ` and `hτ'` tie the regluing automorphisms to $c$ and $c'$: for each $s$ there is a family $c_s$ of $V^\vee$-indexed elements of $k \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s)$, depending on sections over the chart $i_0$ of the special fibre, which is a system of tangent coordinates of the pair consisting of the canonical affine chart map of $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$ and its twist by $\tau_s$ (respectively $\tau'_s$), in the sense of [`AlgebraicGeometry.SmallExtension.IsTangentCoordsOfPairAt`](def/AlgebraicGeometry_TangentCoordsOfPairAt.html#L31) for the small extension ideal $I$, the module $V$, the map $\iota$, the special fibre with its base-changed group law, and the chart indexed by $i_0$ — a condition requiring a map from the spectrum of the thickening $(k \otimes_B \Gamma)\otimes_k (k \oplus V)$ to the special fibre which is a tangent vector of the given pair, whose translate to the unit lands in the chart, and whose chart ring homomorphism has $c_s$ as tangent coordinates — and which satisfies $\sigma_s(c_s(a)(\xi)) = c(a)(\xi)_s$ (respectively $= c'(a)(\xi)_s$) for all $a$ and $\xi$.
--
--   *Coboundary hypothesis.* `hcob` states that for every section $a$ over the chart indexed by $i_0$ of the special fibre and every $\xi \in V^\vee$ there is a Čech $0$-cochain $b \in \check C^0(\mathcal U_k, \mathcal O_k)$ with $d^0 b = c(a)(\xi) - c'(a)(\xi)$; that is, $c$ and $c'$ have the same Čech cohomology class pointwise.
--
--   *Coordinates on $D$ and $D'$.* $G$ and $G'$ are deformations of $F \otimes_B B_1$ to $B$ with commutative underlying laws, $\theta$ and $\theta'$ are systems of formal coordinates for $D.f$ and $D'.f$ with $D.L.\mathrm{IsFormalCoordinates}\ G.F\ \theta$ and $D'.L.\mathrm{IsFormalCoordinates}\ G'.F\ \theta'$ (`hθ`, `hθ'`), both lifting $\theta_1$ (`hl`, `hl'`).
--
--   *Shift datum.* $w$ is a $k$-linear map from $V^\vee$ to the first-order deformation space of $F \otimes_B k$ (first-order cocycles modulo coboundaries), and the hypothesis `h` is $\mathrm{IsShiftBy}\ V\ \iota\ F\ w\ G_0\ G$: there exist $n$, vectors $v_1, \dots, v_n \in V$, first-order cocycles $z_1, \dots, z_n$ for $F \otimes_B k$ and lifts $zl_i$ of $z_i$ to pairs of power series in $B[[X_1, X_2, Y_1, Y_2]]$ reducing to $z_i$ modulo $\mathfrak m_B$, such that $w(\xi) = \sum_i \xi(v_i)\,[z_i]$ for all $\xi \in V^\vee$, and $G.F$ is obtained from $G_0.F$ componentwise by adding $\sum_i \iota(v_i)\, zl_i$.
--
--   The conclusion is $\mathrm{IsShiftBy}\ V\ \iota\ F\ w\ G_0\ G'$: there exist $n \in \mathbb N$, $v : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to V$, first-order cocycles $z : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \mathrm{firstOrderCocycles}(F \otimes_B k)$ and families $zl : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B[[X_1,X_2,Y_1,Y_2]]$ such that the reduction of $zl_{i,l}$ modulo the maximal ideal is the $l$-th component of $z_i$ for all $i, l$; that $w(\xi) = \sum_i \xi(v_i) \cdot [z_i]$ in the first-order deformation space for every $\xi \in V^\vee$; and that for each $l$ the $l$-th power series of $G'.F$ equals the $l$-th power series of $G_0.F$ plus $\sum_i \iota(v_i)\, zl_{i,l}$.
--
--   This is the well-definedness half of the Kodaira–Spencer comparison for regluings: two regluings of the same bare deformation $D_0$ along overlap automorphisms whose tangent coordinate $1$-cocycles differ by a Čech coboundary carry formal group laws lying in the same shift class relative to $G_0$, so the shift attached to a cocycle depends only on its class. It is used in the construction of the Kodaira–Spencer map for these deformations and in the additivity statement for shifts of regluings.
